00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.270 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  i'm tatiana bartel i'm a phd  student here at the helmholtz center in  berlin and i'm working in the field of  structural biology  and our world is a three-dimensional  world so in our research we try to  figure out the three-dimensional  structure of proteins which are the  smallest units of the cell  and by knowing their appearance we can  also try to find out how they work  [Music]  proteins are basically doing everything  in the cell so we also have of course  the dna  which carries the genetic information  but everything that gets this kind of  blueprint into action are the proteins  they take care of all the processes in  the cell  for example they break down our food  they  take care that our immune system  functions they transport substances from  one part to another they take care of  our skin's flexibility and stability  they're responsible for our sight and  smell and so on and so on  so they are the workhorses of our cells  and when we can understand how these  proteins work we can also understand how  the organism works  [Music]  so we want to find out the  three-dimensional structure of proteins  this basically means that we want to  find out the positions of the thousands  of atoms that make up a protein  where they are in relation to each other  in order to get our three-dimensional  structural information  the protein needs to form crystals and  this happens in the way that billions of  proteins need to order  themselves into a repetitive way and  thus we get a crystal that we can then  irradiate with x-ray light  [Music]  besi2 we need for the high intense x-ray  light  the c2 is an electron storage ring it  gives  us a light of a lot of  different energies and we cut out one  portion of the spectrum  the high intensity x-ray light which we  use then to irradiate our  protein crystals these protein crystals  when we radiate them with high intensity  x-ray light  and we get so-called diffraction images  out of them  which are then evaluated with complex  computation  processes and when we calculate our  structure of the protein from these  processes  we can also display them and with the  structural knowledge we have then of the  protein we can try to understand how  they work and then also intervene their  function  [Music]  so we have to get the proteins that we  work with out of some cells  and when we have them we put them in  certain solutions  so that they form these protein crystals  and these protein crystals we then have  to  mount onto a special machine here at  bessie ii  where we can put it directly into the  x-ray beam  this hits then a detector which then has  these diffraction images  and the diffraction images are then  brought to different  computation programs where they are  evaluated and in the end we can solve  the protein structure  and display it as a kind of model  [Music]  as i said before when we know the  appearance of proteins we can  understand how they work and then also  intervene in their function  this is very important for example what  we  did in our project in the spring of this  year  where we investigated a protein of the  of the sars crop2 the main protease  which is called  empro this is a very important protein  that the virus needs to multiply so we  try to intervene there  with our structural knowledge we have of  the protein  we can study different molecules that  intervene the function of it  and this hopefully we can gain an active  substance or even a drug in the end  yes we did find something but it's still  an ongoing investigation so i cannot  talk more about it  [Music]  so um in our case we approached a  lab that we already knew that worked for  several years already on coronaviruses  and  also now on the zarzkov ii and we  made a collaboration with them so we  combine our knowledge that  we and they have and our facility to  get a novel drug in the end so they send  us the protein the m pro  we do here several experiments as i  described before that we  solve the structure with different  molecules  and this all this information then we  passed on to the  other lab again and they continue now  the work  first of all i want to make clear again  that we work here on drugs and not  vaccines  so there are two entirely different  approaches  you administer vaccines for healthy  people to protect them from infection  and drugs you administer to patients to  help them recover from a disease  we basically do the first step in the  whole drug development process  so we take our target protein we search  for starting points to develop a drug  and then the other labs continue this  work to get it into an active substance  in the end  basically also the whole h3p helmet  center in berlin  gathered all the energy we could and we  all already started um  directly to investigate this protein  that i told you before the m  pro so actually we were here in a kind  of shutdown period  but hdb made it possible for us to start  up the machine again in a  time where it usually shouldn't start up  and we could  do our work here as i said we get the  protein from another lab  so we could investigate this protein  with different small molecules  and then we could give this information  back to our collaboration partner  that they continue working on it  [Music]  so for the zaskov2 project we now  collaborate with german laboratories  but for our usual projects we do here we  collaborate with laboratories all over  europe and also beyond for example we  also have a lab that we collaborate  with in israel and mainly we  provide them with expertise from our  side and also help them in the first  steps  for example to find starting points for  drug development or also solving  novel structures  [Music]  so usually in the pharmaceutical  industry they say it takes about 10  years from  finding a target protein to an approved  drug but right now  with a corona virus pandemic  everybody is trying to make this process  go very very fast  and the whole drug development process  can be separated into several steps the  first step is the research phase  where we identify a target protein so a  protein that has an effect on a disease  and then we try to find molecules that  intervene with its function  when we found these we go into another  phase a testing phase  where we test the molecules on different  properties for example if they are toxic  and only then we can go into the longest  phase the clinical phase  where we administer this potential drug  first to healthy volunteers  and then to patients only if we go  through all these steps  successfully we can get this drug  approved by the respective  administration for example in the eu it  would be the european medicine  agency  [Music]  as i said before usually the process  takes about 10 years  so we can only hope that we can cut it  short to a few years  now but it's not really clear how long  it will take  so during the berlin science week we  want to discuss  how we can utilize our knowledge of the  structural information of proteins  to create novel drugs  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you

